dirk NEWS — history of user-visible changes.

Copyright (C) 2026 Oddur Sigurdsson.
See the end of the file for copying conditions.

Please send bug reports and suggestions through the project's issue tracker.


* Noteworthy changes in release 0.3.0 (2026-09-06)

** What is running in a pane

dirk reads the tty's foreground process group, which is exactly what is running
in a pane right now -- the thing a shell sets before it waits for a command and
resets when the command ends.  A pane holds a recognised agent, or is a shell at
its prompt, or is running something else that is neither and is named rather
than guessed at.

An agent run under an interpreter is recognised too.  Installed through npm it
executes as `node`, so its executable says nothing and its identity appears only
in its command line; that is the one case where a command line is read at all.

** Four states

  working   producing output
  blocked   an approval prompt is on screen
  done      stopped, and not looked at since
  idle      stopped, and seen

`done` and `idle` are the same underlying state, and what separates them is
whether you have looked.  That is the whole reason `done` exists: finished work
nobody has noticed is the thing worth showing.  A workspace is seen while it is
focused, and unseen for any moment of work you are not watching.

An agent that is thinking redraws continuously, so silence is what says it has
stopped.  Blocked is read from the rows around the cursor, where a question is
being asked, and matched against markers per agent kind -- a phrase alone is not
enough, since agents write "would you like" in ordinary prose, so a marker
counts only above a menu of numbered answers.

** Attention

The rail carries what is owed, and only when it is not zero.  Clicking a count
goes to the workspace that has been in that state longest.  The agents section of
the nav is ordered the same way.

A notification is sent when an agent blocks and when it finishes unseen, never
for the workspace you are looking at, and at most one per workspace per state
per interval.  Configurable, including off.

** Names

Every agent gets one, taken from the intent of the workspace it is in and unique
among live agents.  It follows the agent and is cleared when the pane stops
holding one.  Nothing addresses an agent by name yet; that is v0.4.

** Naming

A blocked agent is no longer renamed after the question it is asking.  Its title
describes the dialog rather than the work, and the label would stick until the
next intent arrived.

** Known limitations

Sessions still do not survive the terminal closing, which is v0.4 entire.  There
is no scrollback view or copy mode, and keybindings are not configurable.  A
notification cannot yet hand a click back to dirk, which is a packaging question.


* Noteworthy changes in release 0.2.0 (2026-09-06)

** The nav

The sidebar is three lists: layouts, spaces and agents.  The order is the
argument -- layouts are places you go, spaces are where work lives, and agents
are what is asking for you, so attention flows down the column.

A workspace row is two lines.  The first is identity: state, number, name, and
how long since it last said anything.  The second is which checkout it is,
marked when that checkout is a worktree.  A workspace with nothing for its
second line draws one line rather than a blank one.

The same workspace appears under spaces and under agents.  That is not
duplication: spaces answers "what is open, and where" and agents answers "what
needs me", and they are sorted differently for that reason.  The agents list is
ordered by what is owed -- blocked, then finished-and-unseen, then working, then
idle, oldest first inside each -- and the numbers stay attached to the
workspace, so a jump key does not move when the order does.

Each section is allocated height before anything is drawn, so a long list of
spaces cannot push the agents section off the bottom.  A section that fits is
never scrolled.  The selection is distinct from focus and the nav takes keys of
its own while it holds them.  The divider drags.

** Layouts

A layout is a named arrangement of programs, declared in the configuration file
as a tree of panes with sizes, and opened as a space of its own.  A layout with
one program and a five-pane dashboard differ only in how many leaves they have.

A pane a layout declared carries a label, drawn as a rule above it.  When its
program exits the pane keeps its rectangle and its last output, says how it
ended on that rule, and `r` starts it again in the same slot.  The output is
usually the point: a board that prints and exits is a reasonable panel.

Panes run in the directory of whatever was focused when the layout opened, and
closing a layout returns you exactly where you were.

A layout whose programs are not all installed is dropped at startup.  It is all
or nothing, because half a dashboard is not a dashboard.

** Panes

A workspace holds a tree of splits rather than a row of equal panes, so nested
arrangements are expressible and a split in a direction the parent already runs
in keeps the shares equal.  Closing a pane gives its space to its own siblings
and moves nothing else.

** Chrome

Section headings are words rather than letter-spaced capitals.  The bar carries
a quit button at its right, which takes two clicks: it ends every shell and
every agent in the session.

** Known limitations

Unchanged from 0.1.0, less the ones above: sessions still do not survive the
terminal closing, there is no scrollback view or copy mode, keybindings are not
configurable, and the state glyph beside a workspace is still inferred from
whether its pane has published a title rather than read from the agent -- so an
agent waiting on a human is not distinguished from one that is working.  That
last is the whole of v0.3.


* Noteworthy changes in release 0.1.0 (2026-09-06)

This is the first release.  It runs as a single process: closing the terminal
ends the session, and detaching is not implemented.  See "Known limitations".

** Panes

Each pane is a process on a pseudo-terminal whose output is parsed into a
terminal grid and painted into the display.  Colours, bold, italic, underline,
inverse and double-width characters are carried through.  Resizing propagates
both to the kernel and to the parser, so programs inside see the size they are
actually given.

Mouse events reach the program in a pane when that program has asked for them,
and are otherwise interpreted by dirk.  A click means "focus this pane" in a
shell and "click this line" in a program that reports mouse input; there is no
mode to switch between the two.

** The sidebar

Static pages sit above the project tree.  A page is a singleton with no project
— one system monitor, one repository browser — spawned when first opened rather
than kept running in the background.  A page whose program is not on PATH is
dropped at startup rather than failing when it is opened.

Below the pages, a project appears once it has a workspace and disappears when
its last one closes.  Every row is a click target.

** Naming

Workspaces are named from the terminal title the program inside publishes, which
is how coding agents report what they are working on.  No model is called and no
credentials are required.

A label dirk did not write is never overwritten.  Placeholder labels are
adopted.  A reworded title describing the same work is not treated as new
intent, a title must hold still before it is committed, and one workspace is
renamed at most once per interval.  Shell prompts, bare paths, echoed command
lines and the plain repository name are never used as names.

** Configuration

dirk runs with no configuration file.  ~/.config/dirk/config.toml overrides
what it names and leaves the rest alone: the brand shown in the bar, where the
project picker looks, the sidebar width, the scrollback depth, the shell, the
list of pages, and the naming thresholds.

** Known limitations

Sessions do not survive the terminal closing.  There is no scrollback view and
no copy mode.  Splits divide a workspace into equal columns or equal rows and
do not nest.  The state glyph beside a workspace is inferred from whether the
pane has ever published a title, so an agent waiting on a human is not
distinguished from one that is working.  Keybindings are not configurable.

Each of these is recorded in the roadmap; see ROADMAP.md.

